    "result_text": "All done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing, including the two new soft-delete acceptance tests), and there are no other test classes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented owner deletion as a soft-delete across the Spring Boot application:\n\n**Domain model** (`Owner.java`)\n- Added a `deleted` boolean field (column `deleted`), defaulting to `false` so a newly created owner is not deleted.\n- Added `getDeleted()`/`setDeleted(...)` and a convenience `isDeleted()` that reads a `null` stored value as not-deleted.\n\n**Service** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java`)\n- `deleteOwner(...)` now flags the owner `deleted = true` and `save`s it (retaining the row) instead of removing it. The controller still returns `204 No Content` (2xx).\n- The create-time duplicate/identity checks now skip deleted owners: `assertIdentityIsUnique`, `assertHouseholdIsUnique`, and the possible-duplicate detection (`assignPossibleDuplicate`) all filter out `existing.isDeleted()`. So a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only matching owner was soft-deleted.\n\n**API contract** (`openapi.yml`)\n- Added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema (the `OwnerDto` regenerates at build time), so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns the flag.\n\n**Mapper** (`OwnerMapper.java`)\n- Mapped `deleted` via `formatDeleted(...)`, coercing a `null` stored value to `false` so the response always carries an explicit boolean (matching the existing `possibleDuplicate`/`bulkSignupWarning` pattern).\n\n**Schemas** \u2014 added the `deleted BOOLEAN` column to the owners table in all four DB schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, h2 \u2014 runtime default, mysql, postgres).\n\nBehavior now matches the spec: DELETE marks the owner deleted and returns 2xx; GET still returns the owner afterward with `deleted: true`; a freshly created owner has `deleted: false`; and deleted owners are ignored by duplicate/identity checks on create.",
